{% set homeowner_guide %}
<p>Practical advice for Elkmont property owners:</p>

<p><strong>Don't forget outbuildings.</strong> That barn or workshop has a roof that sheds water somewhere. Unmanaged runoff from outbuildings can create problems that affect your whole property — including eroding the ground near your house.</p>

<p><strong>Rural lots drain differently.</strong> Without curbs and storm drains, you're responsible for all the water that lands on (or flows through) your property. Walk your land after a heavy rain: where does water collect? Where does it flow? This information helps design effective drainage.</p>

<p><strong>Longer driveways mean longer routes.</strong> If your driveway is significant, consider where your gutter downspouts are directing water. Is it ending up in the driveway, creating washouts? Redirecting drainage away from traffic areas prevents expensive gravel work.</p>

<p><strong>Older homes need assessment first.</strong> Before installing new gutters on a farmhouse that's been standing for decades, we check the fascia. Years of water exposure often mean hidden rot. Better to discover and fix it during gutter installation than to attach new gutters to failing wood.</p>

<p><strong>Tree debris management:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gutter guards</strong> — Best long-term solution for heavy tree coverage</li>
<li><strong>Professional cleaning</strong> — 2-3 times yearly if no guards</li>
<li><strong>DIY</strong> — Only if you're comfortable on ladders and heights</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>Seasonal timing for <a href="/recent-projects/">Elkmont properties</a>:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>March:</strong> Pre-storm season check before spring rains arrive</li>
<li><strong>June:</strong> Mid-summer inspection after any severe weather</li>
<li><strong>November:</strong> Fall cleaning after hardwood leaf drop</li>
